 "notes": [
  "Source: English Wikipedia season articles ('<year> Major League Soccer season', overall standings table) and 'List of MLS Cup finals', fetched 2026-11-16 and parsed by script; no figures were typed by hand.",
  "Record format is [wins, losses, draws]. 1996-1999 was the shootout era (no draws): wins = regulation wins + shootout wins, losses = regulation losses + shootout losses, draws = 0. From 2000 on draws are real draws (2000-2003 matches could still end drawn after golden-goal overtime; overtime wins/losses are plain wins/losses).",
  "Uneven seasons: in 2001 teams played 26 or 27 matches (season cut short after September 11); in 2020 teams played 18-23 matches (COVID-19; MLS is Back group matches counted toward the regular season and standings were decided on points per game). Totals still balance.",
  "2026 is included in 'seasons': all 30 clubs had completed 34 matches when fetched. It is NOT in 'champions': the 2026 playoffs start 2026-11-19 and MLS Cup 2026 is scheduled for 2026-12-18. Add a 2026 champions entry once it is played.",
  "Lineage - San Jose: per MLS, the 1996-2005 San Jose Clash/Earthquakes and the 2008-on Earthquakes are one club (records and the 2001 and 2003 MLS Cups retained; no seasons 2006-2007). Houston Dynamo is a new club from 2006 even though the 2005 San Jose players and staff moved there.",
  "Lineage - defunct clubs: Tampa Bay Mutiny (1996-2001), Miami Fusion (1998-2001) and Chivas USA (2005-2014) are keyed 'defunct:<name>'. LAFC is not a continuation of Chivas USA; Inter Miami CF is not a continuation of Miami Fusion.",
  "Lineage - MLS-era only: Seattle Sounders FC (2009), Portland Timbers (2011), Vancouver Whitecaps (2011), CF Montréal (2012, as Montreal Impact), Orlando City SC (2015), Minnesota United FC (2017), FC Cincinnati (2019), Nashville SC (2020) start at their first MLS season; NASL/USL/A-League history is excluded.",
  "Renames: Red Bull New York = NY/NJ MetroStars 1996-97, MetroStars 1998-2005, New York Red Bulls 2006-2025; the club began using 'Red Bull New York' as its team name at the start of 2026 (it had been the legal club name since 2006). Wikipedia still titles the club 'New York Red Bulls', including in its 2026 tables.",
  "Renames: Columbus Crew was 'Columbus Crew SC' 2015-2020 (briefly 'Columbus SC' for a week in May 2021 before reverting to Columbus Crew). Chicago Fire FC was 'Chicago Fire' / 'Chicago Fire Soccer Club' through 2019. Houston Dynamo added 'FC' for 2021. Montreal Impact became CF Montréal for 2021. Los Angeles Galaxy became LA Galaxy in 2007. Dallas Burn became FC Dallas for 2005. San Jose Clash became Earthquakes for 2000 (renamed October 1999). No MLS club has changed metro area.",
  "Feed names that differ from the club's full name without any rename (so no 'eras' entry): 'Vancouver Whitecaps' (Vancouver Whitecaps FC), 'LAFC' (Los Angeles FC), 'St. Louis CITY SC' (Wikipedia: St. Louis City SC).",
  "Metros: New England Revolution (Foxborough, MA) -> Boston; Colorado Rapids (Commerce City) -> Denver; FC Dallas (Frisco) -> Dallas–Fort Worth; Red Bull New York (Harrison, NJ) -> New York; San Jose Earthquakes -> SF Bay Area; LA Galaxy (Carson) -> Los Angeles; Real Salt Lake (Sandy) -> Salt Lake City; Inter Miami CF (Fort Lauderdale / Miami) -> Miami; Philadelphia Union (Chester) -> Philadelphia; Minnesota United FC (St. Paul) -> Minneapolis–St. Paul.",
  "Clubs under \"inactive\" have folded but keep their history: they can be picked and ranked, and their cities are credited. Each lists the \"defunct:\" names it covers in \"names\" (its own name when left out)."
